namespace Dakota {

/** \page RuntimeExecutionDesign Runtime Execution Subsystem Design Intent

\section RuntimeExecutionPurpose Purpose

The runtime execution subsystem exists to separate study semantics from
study execution mechanics.

Dakota study components such as Models and Iterators define what a study
means: the variable space, the response space, the algorithm, and the
relationships between parent and child components. The runtime execution
subsystem defines how an already-constructed study is executed with a
particular ParallelLibrary and OutputManager, including communicator
lifecycle, iterator partitioning, and subordinate iterator orchestration.

This subsystem was introduced as part of the move away from
IteratorScheduler toward a runtime model that better supports dependency
injection. The goal is that components can increasingly be constructed as
semantic objects, while execution placement and MPI-aware orchestration live
in a shared runtime layer.

\section RuntimeExecutionPieces Major Pieces

Today the subsystem has three primary abstractions.

StudyRuntime is the top-level runtime-facing handle. It gives library-mode
callers a way to execute a constructed top-level iterator and serves as the
runtime-side factory for subordinate execution helpers.

IteratorContext is the per-subordinate execution handle stored by
components such as NestedModel and the meta-iterators. It preserves the
execution state associated with one child-iterator relationship across the
communicator lifecycle.

IteratorExecutor is the low-level mechanism object that performs most of
the concrete iterator execution work, including partition estimation,
communicator setup/teardown, and scheduled execution behavior.

\section RuntimeExecutionFlow Execution Flow

At top level, a caller constructs semantic Dakota objects and then uses a
StudyRuntime to initialize communicators, execute the top-level iterator,
and free communicators.

For subordinate execution, components such as NestedModel create and store
an IteratorContext. During communicator initialization and execution, that
component calls methods on the IteratorContext to update runtime state,
configure the child iterator, partition ranks, initialize the child
iterator sufficiently to run, and later execute or schedule it. The
IteratorContext carries the long-lived execution state for that child
relationship and relies on IteratorExecutor for the detailed mechanics.

\section RuntimeExecutionBoundaries Boundaries And Intent

The main design boundary is this: semantic study objects should not own
MPI-aware execution policy any more than necessary.

That does not mean Models and Iterators become passive. Components such as
NestedModel still decide when a child study should run, whether execution is
synchronous or queued, and how child results map back into parent
semantics. But the runtime execution subsystem is meant to own the
mechanics of how those studies are actually launched and coordinated.

\section RuntimeExecutionStability Stable Versus Expected To Change

The intended stable idea is the subsystem boundary itself: construction and
semantics on one side, runtime execution mechanics on the other.

What is still expected to evolve is the exact distribution of behavior
between StudyRuntime, IteratorContext, and IteratorExecutor. At present,
StudyRuntime is intentionally thin, IteratorContext is partly a state
holder and partly a forwarding surface, and IteratorExecutor still contains
most of the concrete protocol. Future refactoring may rebalance those roles
without changing the overall subsystem intent.

\section RuntimeExecutionRelatedPages Related Pages

See \ref StudyRuntimeDesign, \ref IteratorContextDesign, and
\ref IteratorExecutorDesign for the component-specific design notes.

*/

} // namespace Dakota
